--TEST--
a `Core\Config::set` is in force only for the request that made it, and the next request on the same core reads the configuration file's value again — `rule:config/the-config-is-an-immutable-snapshot`, M6's *Verify*
--FILE nvs.toml--
# The root file, `rule:config/the-root-is-config-else-nvs-toml-else-the-shipped-defaults` step 2's `./nvs.toml`. `[limits]` is the value
# every request here starts at, `[limits.hard]` is what makes the setter's `set`
# land rather than be refused (`rule:config/three-changeability-classes`), and the `script.spawn` grant is what
# lets this case run more than one request at all (`rule:security/capability-check-at-the-door`).
[capabilities.script]
spawn = true

[limits]
memory = "128M"

[limits.hard]
memory = "512M"
--FILE setter.nvs--
<?nvs
// The request that sets. It starts where the file says, raises `memory` for
// itself, and reads it back — so what the next request answers is being
// compared against a `set` that demonstrably took effect somewhere.
echo "the setter starts at ", Core\Config::get('memory'), "\n";
Core\Config::set('memory', '256M');
echo "the setter now reads ", Core\Config::get('memory'), "\n";
--FILE reader.nvs--
<?nvs
// The next request on the same core: this isolate starts after the setter has
// run to completion, in the same process, and clones the same snapshot. It has
// to read the file's value — a `set` that had reached a process-wide table
// would answer `256M` here, and that is the one failure this case exists to
// catch.
echo "the next request reads ", Core\Config::get('memory'), "\n";
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: tools:config/reading-and-changing-it-from-a-program-core-config
// `rule:config/the-config-is-an-immutable-snapshot`: a request clones the immutable snapshot at start and writes its
// own overlay over that clone, so a `set` moves one request's view and reaches
// no process-wide table — `Core\Config`'s module doc says there is none to
// reach.
//
// Two sequential `spawn script` requests are how that becomes observable from a
// program: `nvs run` is one request and has nothing to compare against, and the
// second `await` below returns only after the first child has finished, so the
// two are ordered rather than concurrent. `output: 'capture'` keeps each
// child's lines out of this one's stream until the parent prints them, which is
// what makes the order in `--EXPECT--` the order they ran in.
var $setter = spawn script "setter.nvs" with(output: "capture");
echo (await $setter)->output;

var $reader = spawn script "reader.nvs" with(output: "capture");
echo (await $reader)->output;

// And the direction the other way: the parent never set anything, and neither
// child's overlay reached it.
echo "the parent reads ", Core\Config::get('memory'), "\n";
--EXPECT--
the setter starts at 128M
the setter now reads 256M
the next request reads 128M
the parent reads 128M
